WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.260
All right.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.860
Thanks everyone for attending.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.960
I know this is the
last talk of the day,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.390
and I appreciate everyone here.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.730
This is less product-oriented
than the others.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.440
So I'm going to
assume you're all here

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.144
as die hard fans of
Production, which is what I am.

00:00:27.144 --> 00:00:28.810
So this morning, we
heard from Joe Kava,

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.420
talking about the hardware and
data center side of the Google

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.530
Cloud platform, and
I'm here to take you

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.930
on a whirlwind tour of how
we support the software side.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.460
My goal is that you walk out of
here understanding how Google

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.570
keeps an infrastructure
like this together,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.040
but that you also have some
ideas that you can take home

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.570
to your teams, both
organizational and tools.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.397
At the end, I will stay up here
if there are any questions.

00:00:51.397 --> 00:00:53.730
And I know it's late and
everyone wants to beat traffic.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.060
I have a set of business
cards, so if you just

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.580
want to grab one and run out
and send me your question,

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.950
I'm happy to answer it later.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:01.610
All righty.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.590
Let's get going.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.410
So first, a bit about me.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.269
I joined Google only a year ago.

00:01:08.269 --> 00:01:10.310
This means I've actually
seen a lot of the places

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:11.600
that you guys are working.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.150
I've worked in
10-person startups,

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.380
I've worked in
100-person startups,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:18.700
I've worked in a retail company
attempting to reinvent itself,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.660
and I also spent almost
a decade at Amazon,

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.240
where I was building internal
tools and automation.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.000
If any of you are familiar
with AWS CodeDeploy,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.190
I designed the internal
tool that's built on.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.154
I know baby pictures
are traditional,

00:01:32.154 --> 00:01:34.320
but I figured you would get
a lot more enjoyment out

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.120
of my 1980s haircut.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.080
So here's a picture
me with Bob Ballard.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.420
This has not shown
publicly before.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.481
Enjoy it while you can.

00:01:42.481 --> 00:01:42.980
All right.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.260
Next, I'm going to talk about
SRE, the What and the Why.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.610
First, I want to tell you
a story about cookies.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.060
Everyone likes cookies.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.370
So let's start with sys admins.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:57.150
What do sys admins
get cookies for?

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.910
Sys admins get cookies
when the site stays up.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.590
Site stays up, we get visitors,
visitors give us money.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.400
Yay!

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.480
Cookies for everyone.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.130
U is for uptime.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.500
How about developers?

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.030
What do developers
get cookies for?

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.870
Developers get
cookies for features.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.550
Release a new feature,
visitors come,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.265
they give us even more money.

00:02:19.265 --> 00:02:19.764
Yay!

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:22.050
Cookies.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.710
Before I continue, I want to ask
you to go home and put together

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.350
an experiment.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.880
Have your developers stop
releasing code for two weeks

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.032
and let me know what happens
to your site availability.

00:02:34.032 --> 00:02:35.490
Maybe you guys work
at a place that

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.480
has a production freeze
for big business reports

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.229
or for holiday stuff.

00:02:40.229 --> 00:02:42.020
The fact is that
production freezes usually

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.100
result in higher uptime.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.860
So you have these two
competing priorities.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.520
Your sys admins get cookies
for keeping the site up,

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.450
your developers get cookies
for releasing features.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.970
So these two teams start
to figure things out.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.250
The sys admins will
get more cookies

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.780
if they could prevent
teachers from going out,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.080
and the developers
will get more cookies

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.240
if they could figure out a way
around the sys admins' rules.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.766
So devs figure out they
can call it a beta.

00:03:08.766 --> 00:03:10.870
It's just a beta, guys, it's OK.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:11.746
Not a big deal.

00:03:11.746 --> 00:03:13.370
And the sys admins
figure out that they

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:16.120
can demand launch reviews
and stretch those out

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.714
for a really long time
if you do it right.

00:03:18.714 --> 00:03:20.130
Developers go, oh,
I have an idea.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.730
We're going to a
percentage flag flip.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:24.330
That's a good idea.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.080
So you end up with this
fighting back and forth,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.580
and sadness, and no cookies,
because all of your teams

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.945
are spending all their
time fighting each other.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:46.040
You get increased addage,
increased risk, chaos, anarchy,

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.720
dogs and cats living together.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.020
So what we want is
to actually take

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.670
all of this whim and
fiat out of the process,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:59.000
have it handled by rules
so teams going have goals

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.710
and work together.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.220
Now, there actually is a
common solution out there

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:06.310
for this to Dev and
Operations problem.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:10.660
There are conferences dedicated
to Dev and Operations.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.120
Anyone have any
idea what those are?

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:13.970
Yeah, DevOps.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:15.840
So problem with
DevOps, I don't know

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.860
if you know the story of the
blind men and the elephant,

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.660
but it's a set a blind men who
go, and one guy feels the trunk

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.570
and it's kind of
leathery and bumpy,

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:28.180
and that's what he decides
the elephant looks like.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.400
Another one feels the
tusks, long, smooth, cold.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.150
That must be with the
elephant looks like.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.940
Another feels the tail.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.300
DevOps is a little
bit like this.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.450
It has a different
meanings everywhere you go,

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.950
different interpretations.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:42.540
So is agile.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.760
So are various other things.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.440
So I'm going to talk about
SRE, because SRE actually

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.740
is well-defined.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.260
In short, this is SRE.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.330
There's two key
phrases in this quote

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.770
from Ben Treynor,
"software engineer,"

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:03.490
and "design and run."

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.540
Software engineer is
critical, because it

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:08.400
turns out services run better
when people who understand

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.792
software also run it.

00:05:11.792 --> 00:05:14.000
They have a deep understanding
of what makes it tick.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.880
They have a deep understanding
of the interactions.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.225
And design and run.

00:05:18.225 --> 00:05:20.770
When you actually design
your production environment

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.780
rather than having it
be a happy accident

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.900
or something some dev did while
hyped-up on some really good

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.320
coffee, right, after drinking?

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.030
You actually have a
better environment.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.910
There are many, many
different aspects to SRE.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.810
I am not going to try
to discuss all of them.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.670
But it's all these activities
that actually make up

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.580
what we call SRE.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.220
So let's say we have
a 1,000 SREs working

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.710
on Google's infrastructure,
network, compute, storage,

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:50.634
et cetera.

00:05:50.634 --> 00:05:52.050
How many of them
do you guys think

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.530
are responsible for Cloud?

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:57.660
Maybe 100 of them, 10%?

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.330
200 of them?

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.650
I'll give you a hint.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:04.860
SREs in every office we have
around the world work on Cloud.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.470
It turns out the answer
is that all of our SREs

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:11.090
who support our infrastructure
also support Cloud.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:13.330
We don't want to
distinguish between what

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.700
makes Google.com run and what
makes Google Cloud Platform,

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.960
and therefore, your
applications run.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.790
We don't want to have
communication overhead

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.210
as the Cloud teams
in the internal teams

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.690
try to communicate.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:28.760
We want to create
one environment that

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.510
helps everything work together.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.060
The rest of my talk is how
we created those teams,

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:38.070
how we run those teams,
and how we do what we do.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.690
So first, how do
we structure SRE

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.810
from an organizational
perspective?

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.290
How do we make sure we hire
devs into a role focused

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:45.440
on production?

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.070
and keep them happy?

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.382
How do we keep the team
valued inside of Google?

00:06:49.382 --> 00:06:51.340
And how do we help our
teams communicate better

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:53.510
and resolve
disagreements with data,

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:57.650
rather than assertions
or power grabs?

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.880
So first, it's all about
leveling the playing field,

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:04.040
having common tools,
having common knowledge.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:07.610
All of our SREs have to pass
a full software interview

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.080
to get hired.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.110
In addition to that,
they have to pass

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:14.822
a non-abstract large
system design interview.

00:07:14.822 --> 00:07:16.530
So in some senses,
although I would never

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.070
tell this to my devs who are
sitting here in front row,

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:22.170
our SREs actually have to be
more skilled than our devs are

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.100
to do the same job.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.510
You need to have a
greater breadth of skills.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:28.670
So it's the same software
skills, but different domain

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:29.955
of application.

00:07:29.955 --> 00:07:32.640
Devs are beholden
to product managers.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.170
They make features.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:37.400
They make you guys happy.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:40.760
SREs are beholden to Production.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:42.330
They're beholden to
making Production

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.850
as good as it possibly can be.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.940
So when both of these
different perspectives

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:49.360
contribute to a design,
the resulting design

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.390
is much stronger.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.200
Here's an example
from an onboarding

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.740
that a friend of mine did.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.671
They evaluated the
team's software

00:07:56.671 --> 00:07:58.920
and they found that there
were a bunch of assumptions.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:03.240
It was going to fail in
production when we hit scale.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.980
The devs had implicitly assumed
a particular type of call

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:07.385
would never fail.

00:08:07.385 --> 00:08:09.510
They had assumed the
distribution of the request

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.030
was uniform.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:14.154
They'd assumed they wouldn't
get hot-spotted by users.

00:08:14.154 --> 00:08:16.695
They'd assumed that all of their
requests were average-sized,

00:08:16.695 --> 00:08:19.186
and they failed
on the two tails.

00:08:19.186 --> 00:08:22.957
But that's what our
SREs bring to the table.

00:08:22.957 --> 00:08:25.290
Then we need to give our devs
a reason not to just allow

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:26.890
operational work to build up.

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:29.820
Because if they're not doing
it, why would they care?

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.630
So we do this by having
our devs actually

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.150
provide the headcount to SRE.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:37.230
So if you have a system that has
a really large open operational

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.606
overhead, you don't
get as many devs.

00:08:39.606 --> 00:08:41.230
You don't get to push
as many features,

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:44.000
because we look at the
headcount in aggregate.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.360
We also have SRE report to an
entirely separate organization.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.990
They have their own VP
separate from the Dev VPs.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.390
This gives them the
authority and power.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.690
It allows them to say
no when Production

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.254
means they need to say no.

00:08:57.254 --> 00:09:00.110
They're not just a
bunch of pager monkeys.

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.150
There's also scarcity.

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.330
The devs say that they
can donate headcount.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.914
SRE doesn't have to accept it.

00:09:07.914 --> 00:09:09.330
They can say,
that's great, but we

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.579
don't think that service is
actually important enough.

00:09:11.579 --> 00:09:14.330
Why don't you keep
supporting it yourself?

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.530
Not every team at
Google has SREs.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:19.940
Cloud does, but not
every other team.

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.840
And not every single
small service in Cloud.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:24.602
It's the important ones.

00:09:24.602 --> 00:09:27.060
So I'll talk a little bit more
about that on another slide,

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:29.325
but the main point is
that SREs are scarce.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.670
The environment, very critical.

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:37.020
So this is how you actually
keep these devs happy,

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.250
running in a Production team.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.240
You need to have at least 50%
of your work being project work.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.280
So that's not on
call, that's not

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.300
tickets, that's not meetings.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:49.440
You actually have to
be doing project work.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.380
And if there's too
much project work,

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:54.330
either the devs give
more headcount to SRE,

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:58.780
or that actual work actually
flows over into the dev team.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.610
Example project work
for SREs, improving

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.730
the latency of a
service by switching

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:06.430
the underlying
database technology,

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.420
writing automation to
speed up deployments,

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.470
and projects that span services.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:13.330
We have a service
internally that

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.267
can be queried by
other services,

00:10:15.267 --> 00:10:17.100
because we make robots
do everything for us,

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.880
not Boston Dynamics-type of
robots but softer robots.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.260
So we have a service you can
query internally that says,

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:26.885
hey, is it safe to
take this machine down?

00:10:26.885 --> 00:10:29.175
Is it safe to take
this rack down?

00:10:29.175 --> 00:10:31.420
Could I take down
this data center

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.670
that actually syncs with the
state of everything else,

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.060
and gives a yes or a no.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.690
And that's maintained by SRE.

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:41.210
And possibly most
critically, our SRE teams

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:42.990
are a volunteer army.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.140
There is no draft.

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:47.250
Not only can you transfer
to another SRE team

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.350
at any time without your
manager's permission,

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:52.200
although this applies to
all devs at Google as well,

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:55.190
but you can transfer into Dev.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.450
So if you're tired of
dealing with Production,

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:59.850
you just really want to sit
down with a product manager

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:03.460
and become best friends,
you can transfer to Dev.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:05.665
You don't have to ask
anyone's permission to do it.

00:11:05.665 --> 00:11:07.310
We actually even
have the reverse,

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:11.150
which is Mission Control, where
devs can try out SRE and see

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:12.740
if they like it.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.320
One of my teams has two mission
controllers on it right now.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:16.444
Really hoping they'll stay.

00:11:16.444 --> 00:11:19.790
If you're watching this,
guys, we want you to stay.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:22.020
So as a result, the
teams are fluid.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.000
People are coming in
and out of the teams,

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.260
sharing experiences,
sharing perspectives.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.940
And finally, we have a budget.

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:32.950
This isn't a money budget
like you're used to.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.220
This is an error budget.

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.860
If I tell you, you have three
nines of service availability,

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.630
I don't want you to go, wow.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:42.500
I'll bet I can push
that to 3 and 1/2,

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:45.590
maybe four nines if I'm
really, really good.

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:48.795
You have 0.1% error budget.

00:11:48.795 --> 00:11:50.940
Go for it!

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.160
If you want to push out
features faster and make

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:57.730
GCP even better, do it,
right up until you run out

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:59.140
of error budget.

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:02.020
If you, instead, would
prefer to have poor tests,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.780
have your software
failing regularly,

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:08.350
and have to rollback constantly,
then you can choose that, too,

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:10.430
but you'll run out of
error budget much faster,

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:13.170
and you actually won't
be able to launch.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:16.420
And so, these are budgets
go in quarterly cycles.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.930
Now, there is an
escape valve for that.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:21.090
It's called Silver Bullets.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:24.430
Some teams have literal,
actual silver bullets,

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:27.800
and a dev can come up and say,
I really, really need to push.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:30.015
I really, really would like
a silver bullet, please.

00:12:30.015 --> 00:12:32.210
And the SRE will
say, no problem.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:36.310
You just have to convince the VP
that you actually need to push.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:38.310
And that little ritual,
which sounds very silly,

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.800
is actually very powerful.

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:43.600
Because it puts it entirely
in the hands of the devs,

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:45.450
they know they have
three silver bullets,

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:47.580
and their VP is the one
that decides whether or not

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.477
it's appropriate to push.

00:12:49.477 --> 00:12:51.810
This even applies if you have
multiple dev teams working

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:54.040
on the service, because
error budgets are

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:57.320
on a per-service
basis, not per-team.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.160
So SRE does not get in the
middle of warring dev teams.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.560
They have to work out amongst
themselves how that error

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:05.940
budget's going to be spent.

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:07.580
So we do have an escape valve.

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:11.460
If all else fails, there is
an irreconcilable dispute,

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:15.530
SRE can off-board-- it's the
opposite of onboarding-- a dev

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:16.650
team.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:19.770
Basically, we've loved
working with you guys.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:21.900
We have irreconcilable
differences.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:24.580
Kind of like an
amicable divorce.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.880
It's rare, but it has happened.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.690
It's a very critical
escape valve

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:32.020
to make sure that you
don't have teams festering

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:34.880
disagreements for long periods.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.920
An example reason
for this might be

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:39.990
if you have, say, a common
database at Google that's

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.860
encouraged for all new
asset-type projects,

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:46.260
we might call it
Spanner, and new projects

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.490
are supposed to use it.

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:50.380
The devs team says, you
know, Spanner's cool and all.

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:52.970
We're going to go build our own.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:56.400
It's very reasonable for the SRE
team to say, I understand that.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:58.880
I disagree with your
reasons, and we actually

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:01.590
don't want to support you if you
go and build your own database

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:05.150
technology, because we think
that's not good for production.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:07.340
SREs are the custodian
of production.

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:10.290
They're the custodian of
the customer experience

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:13.880
for Google.com and for GCP.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:15.429
So we try to avoid this.

00:14:15.429 --> 00:14:17.970
I mean, this may be one or two
times over the history of SRE,

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:19.724
but it has happened.

00:14:19.724 --> 00:14:21.140
And it's not the
end of the world.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:23.910
There are teams
without SRE teams.

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:25.780
They'll survive.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:28.190
SREs support their
teams in a lot of ways.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:29.690
It's not just
about taking pages,

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:31.398
although, I can assure
you that's usually

00:14:31.398 --> 00:14:34.200
what I'm asked very first
when I'm interviewing someone.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:37.220
So you guys get paged?

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:39.660
SRE support is on a spectrum.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:43.579
So it can go, on one end, to
simply chatting with a dev,

00:14:43.579 --> 00:14:45.120
consulting with them
about a service,

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:47.460
doing a little
whiteboard session,

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:50.250
to full-on producing
a design doc together.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.462
Or in the very
extreme, yes, actually

00:14:52.462 --> 00:14:54.295
fully owning a service,
all of the capacity,

00:14:54.295 --> 00:14:58.650
all of the provisioning,
all of the pages.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.250
We don't really think
of pages as the sort

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:03.270
of headliner of what SRE does.

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:06.470
Pages are kind of a way
of keeping you honest.

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.320
So the people responsible
for production

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:10.130
should take the
pages because that

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:11.640
keeps their skin in the game.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.590
And it helps keep the
SRE's skill, expertise,

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:16.470
and perspectives up to date.

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:17.290
All right.

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:20.220
Let's talk a little bit
about what makes things go.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:23.080
Like to talk about the
culture and processes,

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:24.945
but it's not the
scary P processes.

00:15:24.945 --> 00:15:27.070
They're good processes.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:29.360
So in addition to training
and on-call shadowing,

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:32.380
which you guys probably
do at your companies,

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:34.240
we like to run a
role-playing game,

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:38.340
if you will, where one
person is the dungeon master,

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:42.390
and they have a victim,
perhaps a class SRE, level five

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:45.070
lawful neutral alignment,
would be my guess,

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:46.650
onboard the whole team.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:51.460
And they take turns trying to
guess what's actually going on.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:53.500
We run very complex
systems at Google,

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.120
with a lot of
different dependencies.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.120
So it's rare that someone
other than the dungeon

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:01.900
master or the person running the
training session actually fully

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:03.936
knows what the answer is.

00:16:03.936 --> 00:16:05.680
It's good for our
new on-callers.

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:07.404
It let's them actually
test things out

00:16:07.404 --> 00:16:08.570
in a controlled environment.

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:11.690
Some teams actually even have
entire scenarios they can run,

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.210
where they actually
break something in Prod

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:15.982
and have the newbies fix it.

00:16:15.982 --> 00:16:18.500
But it's also good
for the veterans.

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:20.570
When you have very
involved systems,

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.070
it's good to refresh
your knowledge,

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.184
it's good figure,
oh, that's right.

00:16:24.184 --> 00:16:25.350
That's how that thing works.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:29.120
All right.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:31.430
Brief break in the
talk for a quiz.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:35.420
So you're on call for Gmail.

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:36.820
You get a ticket
that says, users

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:39.980
can see other users' emails.

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:41.075
What you do?

00:16:41.075 --> 00:16:43.292
Do you call your team lead?

00:16:43.292 --> 00:16:45.370
Call your VP?

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:46.600
Call for a pizza?

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:49.869
Or do you shut it down?

00:16:49.869 --> 00:16:52.160
I actually ran the test on
this with some non-Googlers.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:54.660
Most people answer
A. The answer is

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:59.600
D. Our on-callers are fully
empowered to do whatever

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:02.900
it takes to protect the
users, to protect information,

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:04.417
to protect Google.

00:17:04.417 --> 00:17:06.000
And if that means
shutting down Gmail,

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.401
if that means shutting
down all of Google.com,

00:17:08.401 --> 00:17:12.390
you, that college hire who's
six months at a school, who just

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:14.319
got paged for your
first time, are

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:16.220
going to be
supported by your VP,

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:21.930
by your SVP for shutting it
down and protecting Google.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:24.300
And we actually don't need
to worry about making sure

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:25.880
that we troubleshoot.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:28.349
Our goal is to restore
service immediately.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:31.250
We have records of the
binary state, we have logs.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:33.260
We'll troubleshoot
when we're awake,

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:36.610
when the devs are in the office,
when everyone's actually there.

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:39.830
We just want to get it
back up and in service now.

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:41.040
All right.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:41.850
Next quiz.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:43.512
Everyone still awake?

00:17:43.512 --> 00:17:45.390
When good software goes bad.

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:47.020
So you have a new dev.

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:49.800
Maybe this is another college
hire, but on the dev side.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:53.950
The new dev pushed code, broke
Google.com, the entire search

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:56.220
engine, for three hours.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:18:01.820
Who's at fault, the dev,
quality of the code reviews,

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:06.430
lack of tasks, lack of
proper canary process,

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:10.360
lack of rapid rollback tools?

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:12.842
Everything except the new dev.

00:18:12.842 --> 00:18:14.550
The new dev shows up
and writes crap code

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.840
that take down your site,
it is not fault of the dev.

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:20.110
It's all the gates between
that dev and working

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:22.130
Prod that should
have stopped him,

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:24.160
and that's where you
should be looking.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:25.650
Yes, people can screw up.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:28.610
But most the time,
human error should never

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.070
have been allowed to actually
propagate beyond the human,

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:33.180
at least not as far as Prod.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:35.740
That brings us to our, probably,
most critical culture point

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:36.830
of all.

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:39.560
Just blameless postmortems.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:41.450
Studies show that most
incidents are actually

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.320
caused by human error.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:45.750
You heard Joe say that they
gotten the incident rate down

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:47.370
really far in the data centers?

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:48.590
That's awesome.

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:52.850
Software has not yet, including
software written by SREs.

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:55.280
We still have as the
highest cause, human error

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:57.270
of one form or another.

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:59.450
And it turns out,
incidents are best

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:02.640
solved when you know
what actually happened.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:05.172
You know the best way to
not know what happened?

00:19:05.172 --> 00:19:07.130
Open every incident with
trying to find someone

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:09.030
to point a finger at.

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:11.260
Because people are really good.

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:13.760
They become really
good hiding and making

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:15.640
sure there's no
trail, and making sure

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.160
you don't actually
know what happened.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:20.200
And you just made your
job 10 times harder,

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:23.140
100 times harder,
1,000 times harder.

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:26.750
So avoiding a blame
culture is critical.

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:30.570
At Google, the general practice
is, whoever screwed up writes

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:32.380
the postmortem.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:35.040
This avoids naming and shaming.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.400
This gives them the
power to make it right.

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:40.480
Multiple people contributed,
which is very common.

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:43.060
Everyone goes in, as
honest as possible,

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:45.290
put in how you screwed up.

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:49.250
We've actually given bonuses
out at All Hands Meetings

00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:52.350
for taking down the
site, because it

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:54.910
was someone who owned up
immediately that they did it.

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:56.665
IRC, oh boy.

00:19:56.665 --> 00:19:58.450
Hang on, guys.

00:19:58.450 --> 00:20:00.620
Rolled it back, and
they got a bonus

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:03.580
for speaking up and taking
care of it so quickly.

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:05.060
And in the event
of major outages,

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:07.870
it's actually not unheard
of for a VP or an SVP

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.500
to kind of ping someone
to say, it's OK.

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:11.140
It's OK.

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:12.556
No one's blaming you've.

00:20:12.556 --> 00:20:13.900
We've got your back.

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:15.830
It'll be all right.

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:18.760
Blameless doesn't mean
there's not names or details.

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:20.305
Obviously, we need
to look into that.

00:20:20.305 --> 00:20:23.720
But it means we're not picking
the people as the reason

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:24.895
things went wrong.

00:20:24.895 --> 00:20:27.020
There shouldn't be any such
thing as an outage that

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:29.220
deserves a firing.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:31.590
We have a detailed
postmortem template

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:34.170
that separates out actions
and to prevent, detect,

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:37.839
and mitigate, because we
want defense in-depth.

00:20:37.839 --> 00:20:39.380
We don't want to
just go, yeah, well,

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:41.430
we did all those things
that we needed to do,

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:43.000
and now, it'll
never happen again,

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:46.125
because, ah, odd are good
it'll happen in some way.

00:20:46.125 --> 00:20:48.500
Maybe you missed something,
or it comes in another angle,

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:49.580
or whatever.

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:51.685
We want to, yes,
prevent outages.

00:20:51.685 --> 00:20:54.090
We also want to detect
them faster and want

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:56.210
to mitigate the impact.

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:58.070
So if something like
this happens again,

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:01.070
it at least won't spread
as far, or last as long,

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:04.550
or affect as many customers.

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:05.866
All righty.

00:21:05.866 --> 00:21:07.890
In the short time
remaining, I'd like

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:11.390
to talk a very little bit about
how we actually do our job.

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:14.620
We really lean heavily
on visualizations.

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:17.340
But first, I want to talk
about the kinds of pages

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:20.270
we like to see as a team.

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:23.700
Pages you know how
to solve are boring,

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:25.462
make you ask if there
isn't a better way.

00:21:25.462 --> 00:21:26.795
Maybe you should invent a robot.

00:21:26.795 --> 00:21:28.340
We invent lots of robots.

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:30.580
We don't like being bored.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:32.490
We don't want to
sit there, dreaming

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:36.280
of a better world in which we
aren't paged by boring things.

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:40.855
If you can write documentation
to fix it, just fix it.

00:21:40.855 --> 00:21:43.180
If you can write down
the steps that clearly,

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:44.990
you could probably
write the automation.

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:46.650
If you can't, go
write something that

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:48.560
lets you write the automation.

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:52.500
Don't be doing things that
robots can do for you.

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:53.544
Pages?

00:21:53.544 --> 00:21:54.960
If you're getting
recurring pages,

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:57.050
stop them from happening.

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:59.290
So the result of this
is that each page we get

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:01.580
is, ideally, really new.

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:03.090
So we don't have boredom.

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:06.210
We definitely
don't have boredom.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.709
This means even our experienced
engineers are probably

00:22:08.709 --> 00:22:11.000
seeing something new every
single time their pager goes

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:12.560
off.

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:15.360
This is a fundamental
change in philosophy.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:19.060
If nothing is routine and
few incidents are repeated,

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:20.680
this means you can't
lean as heavily

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.030
on your previous experience
when debugging the system.

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:27.010
It means you need much
stronger debugging tools.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:30.110
This is not a debugging tool.

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:33.245
This is an obfuscated view of
[INAUDIBLE] messages on my Mac,

00:22:33.245 --> 00:22:35.310
but you get the point.

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:38.205
Standard debugging, like looking
for patterns in log files,

00:22:38.205 --> 00:22:41.270
it doesn't scale if you
don't know what to look for.

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:45.201
And with a platform the size of
GCP, how many machines' logs.

00:22:45.201 --> 00:22:46.700
am I going to look
until I hopefully

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:48.990
find the one that was failing?

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:51.399
So we rely very heavily
on various visual tools

00:22:51.399 --> 00:22:53.190
to help us troubleshoot
unfamiliar problems

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:56.420
and restore service as
quickly as possible.

00:22:56.420 --> 00:22:57.570
Graphing tools?

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:58.220
Oh, sorry.

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:00.840
In the next few slides,
I will have some

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:02.850
Open Source tools mentioned.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:06.540
I'm not advocating these
tools in particular.

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:08.000
We don't use these tools.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.627
These are examples
of what we do.

00:23:10.627 --> 00:23:12.210
So you can use these
as a springboard.

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:13.130
They may be useful.

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:15.850
You may use them to
find other tools.

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:18.259
Graphing is a very traditional
form of visualization.

00:23:18.259 --> 00:23:20.300
It's great when you're
looking at a single thing.

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:23.320
It's even better when
you're looking in aggregate.

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:26.770
Processes that we care about at
Google, there's never just one.

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:29.480
There's billions, and
billions, and billions of them.

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:32.060
So each of these lines,
while they look simple,

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:35.780
are actually pulling from
many different sources

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:37.100
and getting aggregated.

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:39.840
We instrument the heck
out of our binaries,

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:41.540
because, again, with
a novel situation,

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.456
you don't always know
what you're looking for.

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:48.140
Ideally, you have
a framework that

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.310
makes it easy for your
developers to actually export

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:52.470
data into your system.

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:55.630
Developers get bored just
like ops people get bored,

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:59.400
and they have just
as much fun-- none--

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:02.430
writing repetitive
monitoring code.

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:04.759
So come up with a framework,
find a framework, whatever

00:24:04.759 --> 00:24:07.050
you need, so that that is
actually simple for your devs

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.280
to do.

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:11.002
I mentioned this before.

00:24:11.002 --> 00:24:12.960
Possibly most critical
is that there's actually

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:16.410
a huge amount of storage that
we have behind this solely

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:18.010
to store this data,
because we do not

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:21.100
want to be troubleshooting
in the middle of an outage.

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:23.420
An outage is all about
restoring service.

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:25.580
Troubleshooting is what
happens when you're awake.

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:28.029
Our devs are often engaged
in troubleshooting.

00:24:28.029 --> 00:24:29.320
They have the deeper knowledge.

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:31.650
They know where
stuff's going on.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:33.440
So you need to have
this data historically

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:36.215
so you can look at it and not
go, well, we fixed the outage,

00:24:36.215 --> 00:24:37.590
but we lost all
the data that was

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.630
going to tell us what was
going on in the first place.

00:24:40.630 --> 00:24:43.890
This helps keep our unusual
outages as short as possible

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:47.550
and lets us dig into
what goes on later.

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:53.400
Event graphing is really, really
useful for correlating events.

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:55.650
Take advantage of your human
ability to pattern match.

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:57.900
It's actually kind of hard
to write robots to do this,

00:24:57.900 --> 00:24:59.830
although, deep minds
solve [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:03.090
So maybe it will take
this away from us, too.

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:09.190
So this is laying things out
by data center and by time.

00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:11.127
And then-- and I
apologize if that's

00:25:11.127 --> 00:25:13.460
hard to see back there-- the
color is actually the event

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:14.510
type.

00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:16.580
This can help you
find patterns that

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:19.910
aren't single incidents like,
say, a software roll-out that's

00:25:19.910 --> 00:25:22.820
causing cascading failures
throughout the whole stack,

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:25.780
or a cluster of errors
that's repeating together,

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:27.570
or if you see a
latency spike, and then

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:30.349
an error spike immediately
after it, and that's repeated.

00:25:30.349 --> 00:25:32.640
These will all help you get
to the root cause of what's

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:35.850
actually going on.

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:37.330
Visual process tracing.

00:25:37.330 --> 00:25:38.020
I apologize.

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:40.790
I could not actually find a lot
of truly open source options

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:42.790
for this, although there's
a whole bunch of ones

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:43.510
you can pay for.

00:25:46.181 --> 00:25:47.930
When you're looking
at performance issues,

00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:50.550
you sometimes do need to get
down to the process level.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.050
We have a pretty
elaborate framework

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:55.130
that pulls sample
queries into storage

00:25:55.130 --> 00:25:58.072
and actually provides
a full trace of them.

00:25:58.072 --> 00:25:59.530
The advantage to
having a tool that

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:01.250
allows you to do it visually
is, you can actually

00:26:01.250 --> 00:26:03.458
collapse, and expand, and
compare things a lot better

00:26:03.458 --> 00:26:05.575
than if you're looking
at a series of timestamps

00:26:05.575 --> 00:26:09.545
and trying to go, OK, how
many milliseconds is there?

00:26:09.545 --> 00:26:11.670
Maybe you're better at math
in your head than I am,

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:12.260
I don't know.

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:14.810
I'm not good at it.

00:26:14.810 --> 00:26:16.290
Network flows and capacity.

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:18.850
So I work in storage.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:21.040
It turns out that
a lot of storage

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:24.740
is slow problems--
not all, but many--

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:27.030
are root caused in the network.

00:26:27.030 --> 00:26:29.680
And you can be sitting there,
staring at your storage system,

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:33.430
staring at your processes,
and going, what the heck?

00:26:33.430 --> 00:26:35.555
And the problem was way,
way, way far away from you

00:26:35.555 --> 00:26:36.750
and the networking team.

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:39.650
So being able to
quickly see what's

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:42.620
the state, what links are
overloaded, how many packet

00:26:42.620 --> 00:26:45.610
errors are you seeing, is
the link entirely down,

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:47.490
really useful, even if
you are not personally

00:26:47.490 --> 00:26:48.750
responsible for the network.

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:55.270
When all else fails-- yeah,
it's all about logfiles

00:26:55.270 --> 00:26:57.362
again, unfortunately.

00:26:57.362 --> 00:26:58.820
But what you don't
want to be doing

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:01.180
is grepping through these logs.

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:04.757
Having a system that allows you
to have SQL-like queries, where

00:27:04.757 --> 00:27:07.340
you can actually dig into them
more sanely than trying to come

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:10.040
up with the correct regular
expression that will filter out

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:12.750
exactly what you want,
extremely useful.

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:16.660
You want your logs to be easy
to consume, easy to understand.

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:18.780
And there's a Open
Source option there.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:24.396
You can also have Kibana as
a potential visualization.

00:27:24.396 --> 00:27:25.640
All right.

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:27.700
Before I end, there
are two more things

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:30.440
I want to bring
to your attention.

00:27:30.440 --> 00:27:35.560
One is, if this interests
you, we have a book.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:36.542
It's not out yet.

00:27:36.542 --> 00:27:39.000
It comes out in April, and it's
actually getting officially

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.580
announced at SRE Con
next week, but they

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:44.060
told me was allowed
to mention it here.

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:46.590
This is a sample of
some of the topics.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:49.030
This is not at all
an exhaustive list.

00:27:49.030 --> 00:27:53.885
It was written by actual SREs
who are doing actual SRE work.

00:27:53.885 --> 00:27:56.490
We had 70 different
contributors,

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.660
500 years of Google
expertise into it,

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:01.270
and it's a crazy thick book.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:03.420
I'm really excited by it.

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:05.840
So if this was
interesting, all of this

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:09.570
is gone into way more detail,
along with some other stuff

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:10.160
in the book.

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:12.100
I highly recommend it.

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:14.240
Also, I don't know
how many of you

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:17.090
were able to be in for
the announcement for this,

00:28:17.090 --> 00:28:20.080
but we actually have
gotten one of our tools

00:28:20.080 --> 00:28:22.160
to be usable in GCP.

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:26.510
So Stackdriver Error
Reporting, excellent tool, we

00:28:26.510 --> 00:28:28.610
would like to do more of this.

00:28:28.610 --> 00:28:30.419
We have an extensive
ecosystem internally

00:28:30.419 --> 00:28:32.460
that we would like to make
available to our Cloud

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:33.650
customers.

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:37.120
In fact, we would like it to
be available to you, period.

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:38.230
That can be challenging.

00:28:38.230 --> 00:28:40.188
But it's a little bit
easier if we can at least

00:28:40.188 --> 00:28:42.510
make it available on Cloud.

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:44.780
Errors are grouped
and deduplicated

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:46.490
by analyzing their stack traces.

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:48.541
Talked a little bit
about that before.

00:28:48.541 --> 00:28:50.540
And the system knows about
the common frameworks

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:54.440
that you use in your language,
and groups errors accordingly.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:56.090
So please check it out.

00:28:56.090 --> 00:28:57.450
It's very cool.

00:28:57.450 --> 00:29:01.500
It's in beta as on,
I believe, today.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:02.000
All righty.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:04.040
We are not doing questions.

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:05.520
There we go.

00:29:05.520 --> 00:29:07.060
Thank you for your time today.

00:29:07.060 --> 00:29:09.770
I made this short so
you all can slip out.

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:10.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:10.670 --> 00:29:12.220
